# Listen Carefully: Repetition, Energy, and Spectrum

A digit melody is first a finite signal. Begin by editing its samples and moving a temporal-lag control. The two numerical descriptions—large correlation and small shift cost—move in exact opposition.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact identity and proof idea</summary>For a cyclic signal $s$, define $C_s(k)=\sum_i s(i)s(i+k)$, $E(s)=\sum_i s(i)^2$, and $D_s(k)=\sum_i(s(i)-s(i+k))^2$. Expanding each square and reindexing the shifted sum gives $D_s(k)=2E(s)-2C_s(k)$.</details>

## What does a shift actually pair?

A temporal lag changes sequence position. It is not a musical interval. Use the partner display before interpreting any peak.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the sharp equality theorem</summary>Because $D_s(k)$ is a sum of squares, $C_s(k)\le E(s)$. Equality holds exactly when every difference $s(i)-s(i+k)$ vanishes, so the shift fixes the whole signal.</details>

{{visualization:1}}

## Pitch distance is a separate statistic

The interval counter studies differences in values rather than differences in time. Learn more about [musical intervals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interval_(music)) and [autocorrelation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autocorrelation).

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Why the octave bin must be empty</summary>Digits mapped to semitone labels lie between $0$ and $9$, so every absolute difference is at most $9$. An octave requires $12$ semitones.</details>

## From melody to spectrum and back

Complete Fourier coordinates reconstruct every sample whenever Fourier inversion applies. They therefore determine every lag statistic. See the [discrete Fourier transform](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discrete_Fourier_transform) for broader context.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the reconstruction argument</summary>If two signals have the same full Fourier coefficient vector, inverse transformation gives the same value at every position. Equal signals have equal cyclic autocorrelation sums at every lag.</details>

## Reproducible numerical suite

Run the integrated demonstration for identity residuals, exact repetition, Fourier reconstruction error, and the pitch-interval check.

